# PredictorA — Certificate & Licensing Report

## 🏆 Recommended License: Apache License 2.0

### Why Apache 2.0 is the Best Fit for PredictorA

| Criteria | Apache 2.0 | MIT | GPL v3 | AGPL v3 |
|----------|-----------|-----|--------|---------|
| Open source | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Commercial use allowed | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Play Store distribution | ✅ | ✅ | ⚠️ | ❌ |
| Patent protection | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Contributor protection | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Permissive (no copyleft) | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ |
| Used by Android ecosystem | ✅ | — | — | — |

### Key Reasons Apache 2.0 Wins

1. **Google Play Store Compatible**  
   Apache 2.0 is explicitly approved by Google for apps distributed on the Play Store.  
   GPL v3 and AGPL v3 have compatibility issues with Play Store distribution terms.

2. **Patent Protection for Contributors**  
   Apache 2.0 includes an express patent grant — contributors grant users a patent  
   license to their contributions. This protects both you and contributors from  
   patent trolls. MIT does not have this protection.

3. **Industry Standard for Android**  
   AOSP (Android Open Source Project) itself uses Apache 2.0. Most major  
   Android libraries (Retrofit, OkHttp, Hilt, Compose) are Apache 2.0.  
   Using the same license ensures clean compatibility.

4. **Commercial Fork Friendly**  
   Others can build commercial products on top of PredictorA without being  
   forced to open source their additions (unlike GPL). This encourages adoption  
   and contribution from companies.

5. **Attribution Required**  
   Users must retain copyright notices and the license, giving your project  
   and contributors proper credit.

